# Cicada hyprlock stack.
#
# The duress check runs FIRST and with expose_authtok so it sees the typed
# secret before pam_unix does. It is `optional`, so it can never lock you out:
# if the script is missing, unreadable, or the hash file does not exist, PAM
# carries on to the normal auth below exactly as if this line were absent.
#
# Enrol the session duress password with: cicada-duress-enroll --session
#
# On `seteuid`, and why it is not enough here:
#
#   pam_exec runs the hook inside whatever process asked for the password, and
#   `seteuid` makes it run with that process's EFFECTIVE uid. Under sudo that is
#   root, because sudo is setuid. hyprlock is NOT setuid — Arch ships it 0755
#   and it authenticates through unix_chkpwd like any unprivileged program — so
#   here the hook runs as the desktop user no matter what this line says. It
#   could not read the 0400 verifier, let alone erase a keyslot, and it exited 0,
#   which is exactly what a wrong guess does. The credential was inert at the
#   lock screen, which is the room it was written for.
#
#   The flag stays because it is correct and free for any setuid caller. What
#   actually makes the lock screen work is cicada-duress.socket: the hook, when
#   it is not root, forwards the candidate to a root handler over that socket.
#   Verified end to end on a real kernel, as an unprivileged uid, by
#   tests/linux/duress.sh.
auth       optional   pam_exec.so seteuid expose_authtok quiet /usr/local/bin/cicada-duress-check
auth       include    system-auth
account    include    system-auth
password   include    system-auth
session    include    system-auth
